"What is saltatory conduction, and how does it differ from non-saltatory conduction in nerve fibres?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is saltatory conduction, and how does it differ from non-saltatory conduction in nerve fibres?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Saltatory conduction is a type of nerve impulse propagation that is faster and more efficient than non-saltatory conduction. It occurs in nerve fibres called meduated fibres, which have a protective covering called a myiculum. Mymiculum is a lipid-rich sheath that surrounds the axons of these fibres, and it has gaps called nodes.

In saltatory conduction, the nerve impulse propagates in a jerky, orsaltatory*, manner, traveling from one node to the next. This is because the mymiculum acts as an insulator, preventing the flow of ions across the axon surface. As a result, the nerve impulse must be generated at the nodes, where the mymiculum is not present.

Non-saltatory conduction, on the other hand, occurs in non-meduated fibres, which have no mymiculum. In this type of conduction, the nerve impulse propagates continuously along the entire length of the axon. This process is slower and less efficient than saltatory conduction, as the ions must flow along the entire axon surface.

In summary, saltatory conduction is characterized by the rapid, discontinuous propagation of nerve impulses through the nodes of meduated fibres, while non-saltatory conduction is characterized by the continuous, slower propagation of nerve impulses along the entire length of non-meduated fibres.
