What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The definition of whole body oxygen delivery is the total amount of oxygen that is transported to the body's tissues and cells per unit of time. It is a measure of the efficiency of the respiratory and circulatory systems in delivering oxygen to the body's metabolically active tissues.

To calculate whole body oxygen delivery, you can use the following formula:

VO2 = VOA × (1 - PaCO2 / PaO2)

Where:
VO2 = Whole body oxygen consumption (in mL/min)
VOA = Volume of air available to the body (in mL/min)
PaCO2 = Partial air pressure of carbon dioxide (in mmHg)
PaO2 = Partial air pressure of oxygen (in mmHg)

This formula is based on Fick's equation, which relates the rate of gas transfer across a membrane to the partial pressures of the gases on opposite sides of the membrane. In this case, the Fick equation is applied to the lungs, where oxygen is transferred from the air in the alveoli to the blood in the capillaries.

It is important to note that this formula is a simplified representation of the complex process of oxygen delivery to the body. In clinical practice, whole body oxygen delivery is often measured using indirect methods, such as indirect calorimetry or arterial blood gas analysis.
