","Hemoglobin E (HbE) disorders are a group of hemoglobin-related disorders caused by a mutation in the β-globin gene, resulting in the production of an abnormal form of hemoglobin (HbE). HbE is a variant of hemoglobin that has a glutamate residue replaced by a valine residue at the β-chain position 5. This mutation leads to reduced oxygen-binding capacity and structural instability of the hemoglobin molecule.

Key features of Hemoglobin E disorders include:

1. **HbE-associated anemia**: The primary feature is mild to moderate anemia due to reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. This is because HbE is less effective at binding and releasing oxygen compared to normal hemoglobin (HbA).

