","The staging of oral cavity cancer involves the evaluation of various factors, including physical examination, imaging, and biopsy. Radiographic findings alone are insufficient to diagnose extranodal extension (ENE) in the oral cavity cancer staging process. This is because imaging techniques may not provide sufficient confidence in detecting microscopic ENE, which is a critical factor in determining the extent of the cancer and its prognosis.

To accurately diagnose EENE, a biopsy of the tumor and surrounding tissue is necessary. This biopsy can be done during a physical examination or during a more invasive procedure, such as a biopsy of the tumor or the surrounding lymph nodes. The biopsy sample is then examined by a pathologist to determine if the tumor has spread outside the lymph nodes.

In summary, while radiographic findings can provide valuable information about the size and location of the tumor, they are not sufficient to diagnose ENE. A biopsy is necessary to confirm the presence of extranodal extension and guide the staging process.
