The relationship between vapor pressure and PEL is that substances with higher vapor pressures generally have lower PEL values. This is because substances with higher vapor pressures can more easily evaporate and reach dangerous concentrations in the air. In the case of methanol, ethanol, and MTBE, the vapor pressure follows the order methanol > ethanol > MTBE, and the PEL values follow the order methanol (200 ppm) < ethanol (1000 ppm) < MTBE (100 ppm). This trend indicates that methanol, with the highest vapor pressure, has the lowest PEL value, as it can more easily reach dangerous concentrations in the air. Ethanol, with a lower vapor pressure, has a higher PEL value, and MTBE, with the lowest vapor pressure, has the highest PEL value.

