<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample, we need to know the volume of the added standard solution and the concentration of the standard solution. However, the problem does not provide this information. 

If we assume the volume of the added standard solution is \( V \) and its concentration is \( C \), we can set up the following equation using the principle of mass balance:

\[
\text{Mass of compound in the sample} = \text{Mass of compound added from the standard solution}
\]

The mass of the compound in the sample can be calculated as:

\[
\text{Mass of compound in the sample} = \text{Concentration of compound in the sample} \times \text{Volume of sample}
\]

\[
= 0.0125 \, \text{M} \times 100 \, \text{mL} = 1.25 \, \text{mmol}
\]

The mass of the compound added from the standard solution can be calculated as:

\[
\text{Mass of compound added from the standard solution} = \text{Concentration of standard solution} \times \text{Volume of standard solution added}
\]

\[
= C \times V
\]

Setting the two mass equations equal to each other:

\[
1.25 \, \text{mmol} = C \times V
\]

Solving for \( C \):

\[
C = \frac{1.25 \, \text{mmol}}{V}
\]

Thus, the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample is:

\[
\boxed{\frac{1.25 \, \text{mmol}}{V}}
\]

Without the volume of the added standard solution (\( V \)) and its concentration (\( C \)), we cannot provide a numerical answer for the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample.
