2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the formation of hydroxide ions (OH⁻) from the NaOH.
3. The solution may release heat due to the exothermic nature of the reaction.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s)} + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)}
\]
Here, the benzoic acid donates a proton (H⁺) to the hydroxide ion (OH⁻) from NaOH, forming water (H₂O) and the sodium salt of benzoic acid, sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)). 

Final answer: $\boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s)} + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)}}$

