","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: chain isomerism and functional group isomerism.

1. Chain isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the carbon atoms in the molecule are arranged in different orders. For C4H10O, the possible chain isomers are:

   a. Butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH): The hydroxyl group is attached to the first carbon atom.
   b. Butan-2-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3): The hydroxyl group is attached to the second carbon atom.

2. Functional group isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the molecule has the same molecular formula but different functional groups. For C4H10O, the possible functional group isomers are:

   a. Butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH): Contains an alcohol functional group (-OH).
   b. Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3): Contains a ketone functional group (-CO-).

In summary, the two types of isomerism in C4H10O are chain isomerism (different carbon chain arrangements) and functional group isomerism (different functional groups).
