","To determine which sulfides precipitate when \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is added to the given mixture, we compare the solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) of the sulfides formed with the concentration of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) in the solution. The \( K_{sp} \) values for the sulfides are as follows:

- \( \text{MnS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-14} \)
- \( \text{NiS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-21} \)
- \( \text{CuS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-29} \)
- \( \text{HgS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-54} \)

The \( K_{sp} \) values indicate the solubility of the sulfides in water. Smaller \( K_{sp} \) values correspond to less soluble sulfides. Here, \( \text{HgS} \) is the least soluble sulfide, followed by \( \text{CuS} \), then \( \text{NiS} \), and finally \( \text{MnS} \).

When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is passed into the solution, it forms \( \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ \) and \( \text{S}^{2-} \). The \( \text{S}^{2-} \) ions react with the metal ions to form sulfides. The sulfides with lower \( K_{sp} \) values will precipitate first because they are less soluble.

Thus, the sulfides that will precipitate are:

1. \( \text{HgS} \) (least soluble)
2. \( \text{CuS} \)
3. \( \text{NiS} \)

\( \text{MnS} \) is more soluble and will remain in solution.

Final answer: \(\boxed{\text{HgS}, \text{CuS}, \text{NiS}}\)
