","The terms ""isotopologues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitution. However, they differ in the context of which isotopic substitution is being discussed:  
1. **Isotopoplogues**: This term refers to molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( ^1H_2O \) and \( ^2H_2O \) (i.e., \( H_2O \) and \( D_2O \)) are isotopologues because they differ only in the isotopic substitution of hydrogen to deuterium.  
2. **Isotopomers**: This term refers to molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of atoms in the same position. For example, \( ^1H^{13}C^{16}O \) and \( ^1H^{12}C^{16}O \) are isotopomers because they differ only in the isotopic substitution of \( ^1C \) to \( ^{13}C \).  

Thus, the key difference is that isotopologues can have isotopic substitutions at different positions in the molecule, while isotopomers must have substitutions at the same positions.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isotopologues: Isotopic substitution at any position.} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Isotopic substitution at the same position.}
\end{aligned}
}
