In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
A σ molecular orbital is characterized by electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis, resulting from head-on overlap of atomic orbitals. In contrast, a π molecular orbital has electron density concentrated on either side of the internuclear axis, arising from sideways overlap of atomic orbitals. This difference in electron density distribution affects the type of bonding: σ bonds are generally stronger and more stable, while π bonds are weaker and more reactive, often involved in double and triple bonds.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis connecting the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a symmetrical electron density distribution.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis connecting the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a side-by-side overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a nodal plane (zero electron density) along the axis.  

Thus, σ bonds are stronger and more stable than π bonds because they have greater electron density directly between the nuclei, leading to stronger electrostatic attraction.  

\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the axis, no nodal plane along the axis; π orbital: electron density above/below the axis, nodal plane along the axis.}}

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

