","1. **Convert the speed of the train to meters per second**:  
   \( 45 \text{ km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{1 \text{ km}} \times \frac{1 \text{ hr}}{3600 \text{ s}} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} \).

2. **Calculate the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger**:  
   \( 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 9 \text{ m/s} = 2.5 \text{ m/s} \).

3. **Determine the length of the train**:  
   The time \( 25 \) seconds is the time it takes the train to cover the distance equal to its length plus the 150 m distance from the jogger.  
   \[
   \text{Length of train} = 2.5 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} - 150 \text{ m} = 62.5 \text{ m} - 150 \text{ m} = -87.5 \text{ m}.
   \]
