To generate 50 sample realizations, we use a random number generator to generate 50 independent sets of 11 independent standard normal variables. The recursion equation is then applied to each set to obtain the 11 values of the Wiener process for each realization. The results are stored in a 50 × 11 matrix, where each row represents a realization.  

(b) The 8 realizations are plotted as lines, with time on the x-axis and the values of the Wiener process on the y-axis. The nonstationary character of the process is evident from the fact that the lines are not parallel and their slopes change over time. This is because the Wiener process has a mean rate of change of zero, but the variance increases with time.  

