","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in the best-case case of QuickScreen is:
\[
T(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
T\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + T\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Splitting the Input**: The base time is \(T(1) = 0\) because a single element is already sorted. For \(n > 1\), the first step is to choose a pivot element and split the input into two equal-sized sub-lots, each of size \(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor\) and \(\lceil n/2 \rceil\).
2. **Sub-Case Comparisons**:
   - The number of comparisons to compare the pivot with the elements of its sub-lots is \(n - 1\).
   - The number of comparisons in the two sub-lots is \(T(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor)\) and \(T(\lceil n/2 \rceil)\), respectively.
3. **Combining Results**: The total number of comparisons is the sum of the above two parts:
   \[
   T(n) = T\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + T\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1.
   \]
   This recurrence is derived from the fact that the number of comparisons in the original problem is the sum of the number of comparisons in the two sub-locals plus the number of comparisons to compare the pivot with the elements of its sub-lots.

The time of this recurrence is \(O(n \log n)\), which is the same as the time of the original QuickScreen algorithm.
