","To calculate the activation energy \( E_a \), we use the Arrhenius equation:

\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:

\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

Taking the difference in the natural logarithm of the rate constants at two temperatures:

\[
\ln k_2 - \ln k_1 = \frac{E_a}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Rearranging to solve for \( E_a \):

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln k_2 - \ln k_1}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}} \cdot R
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln (10^4) - \ln (10^{-1})}{\frac{1}{277.8 \, \text{K}} - \frac{1}{1111.1 \, \text{K}}} \cdot 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{10 - (-1)}{\frac{1}{277.8} - \frac{1}{1111.1}} \cdot 2
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{11}{0.00358} \cdot 2
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{22}{0.00358}
\]

\[
E_a = 6160 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy is \(\boxed{6160 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}}\).
