"Calculate the change in Gibbs free energy when 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas at a constant temperature of 298 K and a pressure of 1 bar, given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water to be -237.1 kJ/mol.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Calculate the change in Gibbs free energy when 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas at a constant temperature of 298 K and a pressure of 1 bar, given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water to be -237.1 kJ/mol.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy (ΔG) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔG = ΔG(products) - ΔG(reactants)

For the reaction of 2 moles of hydrogen gas (H₂) with 1 mole of oxygen gas (O₂) to form 2 moles of water (H₂O), the balanced chemical equation is:

2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l)

The standard Gibbs free energy of formation (ΔG°) of water is given as -237.1 kJ/mol. Since the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of elements in their standard state is zero, the ΔG° for H₂(g) and O₂(g) is 0.

Now, we can calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction:

ΔG = [2 × ΔG°(H₂O)] - [2 × ΔG°(H₂(g)) + ΔG°(O₂(g))]
ΔG = [2 × (-237.1 kJ/mol)] - [2 × 0 + 0]
ΔG = -474.2 kJ/mol

So, the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is -474.2 kJ/mol.
