","The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{\pm 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}}\). This is derived from the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle, which states that the product of the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\)) and the uncertainty in time (\(\Delta t\)) must satisfy:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\]
Here, \(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi}\) is the reduced Planck constant. For \(\hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J} \cdot \text{s}\), the lifetime \(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\), the uncertainty in energy is:
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J} \cdot \text{s}}{2 \times 1.054 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.12 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]
Converting this to MeV:
\[
\Delta E = \frac{5.12 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J/MeV}} = 3.19 \times 10^1 \text{ MeV} = 31.9 \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the typical range of outcomes is \(\pm 31.9 \text{ MeV}\), but this is much larger than the rest energy of the Σ⁺. Instead, the uncertainty in the rest energy is dominated by the uncertainty in the rest mass, which is given by:
\[
\Delta m = \frac{\Delta E}{c^2}
\]
For \(c = 3.0 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}\), the uncertainty in the rest mass is:
\[
\Delta m = \frac{3.19 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}}{(3.0 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})^2} = 3.38 \times 10^{-29} \text{ kg}
\]
Converting this to MeV:
\[
\Delta m = \frac{3.38 \times 10^{-29} \text{ kg}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ kg/MeV}} = 2.18 \times 10^{-16} \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{\pm 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}}\).
